WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.400
A king of New York City's skate scene,&nbsp;
an '80s teen queen destined for fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.200
and an "animal" who left pro sports for&nbsp;
Japanese TV. They've been gone for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.729
but these actors haven't been forgotten.

00:00:12.897 --> 00:00:18.360
Rob Knox's portrayal of the obnoxious Marcus Belby&nbsp;
in "Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince" stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:23.820
as his first and only major part in a motion&nbsp;
picture. Belby is a Ravenclaw student and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.900
candidate for the Slug Club, a meeting&nbsp;
of Hogwarts' best and brightest pupils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.000
hosted by Professor Horace Slughorn. Knox played&nbsp;
the part of Belby with a sweet vulnerability.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.320
Before he could begin filming his planned&nbsp;
role in "Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.360
Part 1," Knox died after an early morning&nbsp;
fight outside the Metro Bar in Southeast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.460
London. Knox had just wrapped his scenes for "The&nbsp;
Half-Blood Prince." He was stabbed after stepping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.140
in to thwart an attack on his younger brother,&nbsp;
Jamie. In the weeks leading up to the stabbing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.060
his killer, Karl Bishop, had altercations&nbsp;
with Knox and his friends. Bishop wound up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:03.360
with a life sentence in prison. Rob Knox&nbsp;
died on May 24, 2008, at the age of 18.

00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:08.880
For Marvel movie fans, Powers Boothe is a&nbsp;
familiar face. The actor portrayed Gideon Malick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.780
the villainous former leader of Hydra, in&nbsp;
"The Avengers" and "Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:18.900
But the character actor enjoyed a lengthy film and&nbsp;
television career long before his MCU role. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.480
years spent perfecting his craft on the stage,&nbsp;
Boothe made his film debut in 1980's "Cruising."

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.180
The '80s saw Boothe deliver strong performances&nbsp;
in films like "Red Dawn" and "The Emerald Forest."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:36.087
Through four decades as an actor, Powers Boothe&nbsp;
appeared in over 60 films and television shows.

00:01:36.087 --> 00:01:38.787
"Well my full attention is at your disposal."

00:01:38.787 --> 00:01:41.640
Boothe's last film was 2014's "Sin City:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.860
A Dame to Kill For," the follow-up to Robert&nbsp;
Rodriguez and Frank Miller's 2005 hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:53.815
"Sin City." He died at the age of 68&nbsp;
on May 14, 2017 from pancreatic cancer.

00:01:53.978 --> 00:01:56.400
Matthew Garber played the younger Banks child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.080
Michael, in the original "Mary Poppins," and&nbsp;
was only eight years old when he took the role.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.820
The eldest of two boys, Garber began acting&nbsp;
with the encouragement of Shakespearean actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.500
Roy Dotrice. Garber's film debut came in&nbsp;
Disney's "The Three Lives of Thomasina"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.700
when casting directors fell in love with&nbsp;
the boy's fun, exuberant personality.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:22.020
After "Mary Poppins," Garber starred in a final&nbsp;
Disney film, 1967's "The Gnome-Mobile." In 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.180
a 21-year-old Garber caught hepatitis while&nbsp;
traveling through India. Although he returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.500
to the United Kingdom for treatment, it&nbsp;
was too late and the disease had affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:38.324
his pancreas. On June 13, 1977, Matthew&nbsp;
Garber died of complications from hepatitis.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:43.860
Alexis Arquette was born into Hollywood royalty.&nbsp;
Her father, Lewis Arquette, was an actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.420
director, and her mother, Brenda "Mardi"&nbsp;
Nowak, was an actor and activist who taught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:54.540
acting classes and ran a theater. Her siblings,&nbsp;
Rosanna, Patricia, and David Arquette, would each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:59.820
carve a successful path for themselves to acting&nbsp;
stardom. At 12 years old, Alexis took the plunge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.680
into professional acting, starring in a music&nbsp;
video for the song "She's a Beauty" by The Tubes.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.720
Born Robert Arquette, Alexis knew she was&nbsp;
transgender from a young age. Arquette had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:14.160
sex reassignment surgery in 2006&nbsp;
and supported other celebrities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.110
like Chaz Bono, who wanted to do the same.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:19.509
"I'm happy to speak about it to anyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.509 --> 00:03:24.600
even if it's somebody who I don't know.&nbsp;
But I certainly have boundaries, limits."

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.540
In the 1980s, she broke ground for transgender&nbsp;
youth. At 19, she played a trans sex worker in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.360
the 1989 film "Last Exit to Brooklyn." Arquette&nbsp;
starred in over 40 films through her prolific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.760
career — although most were small, independent&nbsp;
movies. In the 1990s, Arquette appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.780
in supporting roles in "She's All That,"&nbsp;
"The Wedding Singer," and "Pulp Fiction."

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:52.560
After contracting HIV in 1987, Arquette went&nbsp;
through long periods of poor health. In 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.360
she decided to live once more as a man. But&nbsp;
Arquette's increasing health decline could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:04.080
not be reversed. On September 11, 2016, at 47,&nbsp;
Alexis Arquette died after being placed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.000
medically-induced coma due to HIV complications.&nbsp;
The official cause of death was cardiac arrest.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.660
George Gaynes enjoyed a storied acting career.&nbsp;
Before emigrating to the U.S. in his 30s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.340
Gaynes served in World War II as a member&nbsp;
of the Royal Netherlands Navy. He trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.380
at the legendary Actors Studio in New York&nbsp;
City in the 1950s and began his career on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:30.540
Broadway. Gaynes appeared in classic 1970s&nbsp;
series like "The Six Million Dollar Man" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.240
"Columbo," often playing minor, supporting&nbsp;
roles. He acted in 10 films in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.900
including "Tootsie," "Dead Men Don't&nbsp;
Wear Plaid," and "Police Academy."

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:43.980
George Gaynes warmed kids' hearts in&nbsp;
his long-lasting television portrayal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:49.260
of Henry Warnimont, the kindly foster dad&nbsp;
of Punky on the NBC series "Punky Brewster."

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:54.360
Gaynes married his wife, television&nbsp;
actor Allyn Ann McLerie, in 1953,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:59.100
and they remained together until his death.&nbsp;
Gaynes made his last film appearance alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.500
Ashton Kutcher and Brittany Murphy in&nbsp;
2003's "Just Married." On February 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:10.459
2016, at the well-seasoned age of&nbsp;
98, Gaynes died of natural causes.

00:05:10.846 --> 00:05:16.260
Theresa Saldana made her film debut in 1978's&nbsp;
"I Wanna Hold Your Hand" and rose to fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:21.440
with her role in the Academy Award-winning film&nbsp;
"Raging Bull." Saldana played Lenora LeMotta, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.280
sister-in-law of protagonist Jake LeMotta.&nbsp;
Saldana quickly garnered recognition for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.780
her acting talent, later scoring a role in&nbsp;
"Defiance," which also starred '80s star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.460
Jan-Michael Vincent, and playing Rachel&nbsp;
Scali on the CBS series "The Commish."

00:05:35.641 --> 00:05:40.501
"Big house on the hill, that's not the&nbsp;
American dream. We're the American dream."

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:42.533
"Damn straight."

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:48.540
On March 15, 1982, Arthur Richard Jackson, a&nbsp;
Scottish drifter who had been stalking Saldana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.613
approached the actor in front of her home&nbsp;
and stabbed her with a hunting knife.

00:05:52.613 --> 00:05:55.260
"I did call the police on March 8th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.100
and they said that they couldn't send anyone&nbsp;
out because there wasn't enough manpower."

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.200
The actor survived the violent attack, but&nbsp;
was left with severe wounds and spent four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.740
months in the hospital. Jackson&nbsp;
served over a decade in prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.740
but continued to threaten&nbsp;
Saldana from his jail cell.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:16.260
After her attack, Saldana founded the advocacy&nbsp;
group Victims for Victims. She lobbied Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.820
to help establish California's 1990&nbsp;
anti-stalking law and the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:27.240
1994 Driver's Privacy Protection Act. Saldana&nbsp;
wrote a memoir about her experiences called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.620
"Beyond Survival" and played herself in&nbsp;
the television film "Victims for Victims:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:38.040
The Theresa Saldana Story." Saldana continued&nbsp;
to act on television until 2003 and on the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:44.880
screen through 2004. On June 6, 2016, at&nbsp;
the age of 61, Saldana died of pneumonia.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.260
Ron Lester is probably best known for playing&nbsp;
Billy Bob, the fun-loving offensive guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:54.267
on the West Canaan, Texas high school&nbsp;
football team in 1999's "Varsity Blues."

00:06:54.267 --> 00:07:00.000
"Hey Mox! You skinny ass b----! Let's roll!"

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.160
A Georgia native, Lester began his acting career&nbsp;
in commercials and appeared in a music video for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.080
the country band Little Texas. He left Georgia&nbsp;
for the sunny skies of Los Angeles to pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:16.380
acting and stand-up comedy full-time. From&nbsp;
1997 to 2002, Lester appeared in five films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.120
including "Good Burger" and "Not Another Teen&nbsp;
Movie." He made his last film appearance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.740
Roger Hardwick in 2015's "Racing Legacy"&nbsp;
— a film he also wrote and directed.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.560
Lester underwent gastric bypass surgery in&nbsp;
2000, dropping over 300 pounds from his heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:36.840
frame. But in 2015, he began to experience&nbsp;
severe liver and kidney problems from which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:41.820
he never recovered. At the beginning of&nbsp;
June 2016, he began hospice care. Ron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:47.805
Lester died of kidney and liver failure&nbsp;
on June 17, 2016. He was 45 years old.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.680
For over half a century, David Huddleston&nbsp;
graced our cinemas and small screens. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.640
acting career showed impressive range,&nbsp;
and his character roles endeared him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.340
audiences, but Huddleston wasn't a household name.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:05.040
You may know David Huddleston from films like&nbsp;
"Blazing Saddles" and the 1971 made-for-TV movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.600
"Brian's Song," as well as his portrayal of&nbsp;
the titular character in "The Big Lebowski.".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.100
On television, Huddleston's roles ranged from&nbsp;
"Gilmore Girls," where he portrayed the beloved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:18.060
eccentric mayor Harry Porter, to "Bewitched,"&nbsp;
where he played several characters.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:22.920
Huddleston grew up in post-Great Depression&nbsp;
rural Virginia and fell in love with acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.540
in his youth. His first film appearance&nbsp;
came via an uncredited role in 1963's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:32.700
"All the Way Home." His last movie&nbsp;
was 2014's "Locker 13." On August 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.523
2016, David Huddleston died at 85&nbsp;
due to heart and kidney disease.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:42.540
"Okay. Who gets the privilege?"

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:47.520
Another respected character actor, Jon Polito&nbsp;
co-starred with David Huddleston in "The Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.200
Lebowski," where he played P.I. Da Fino.&nbsp;
Chances are you've seen many of Polito's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:57.180
movie turns as a mobster or cop — two of the&nbsp;
actor's mainstay characters. His outstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:02.340
film resume includes four other Coen Brothers&nbsp;
films: "Barton Fink," "The Hudsucker Proxy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.740
"The Man Who Wasn't There," and the epic "Miller's&nbsp;
Crossing." He also played Detective Steve Crosetti&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:13.920
on the NBC television show "Homicide:&nbsp;
Life on the Street," from 1993 to 1994.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:20.160
Polito studied theater at Villanova University. By&nbsp;
the time he was 30, he began regularly working in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.960
Hollywood productions. Although his roles were&nbsp;
often typecast, Polito uniquely crafted each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.480
character. In a time when many actors remained&nbsp;
closeted, Polito was openly gay and married his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.780
longtime partner, Darryl Armbruster, on&nbsp;
October 16, 2015. On September 1, 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:43.080
less than a year after his wedding, Polito&nbsp;
died due to multiple myeloma. He was 65.

00:09:43.246 --> 00:09:46.560
Brad Lesley's name may have more&nbsp;
recognition with hardcore baseball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.960
fans than with moviegoers. Known as "The Animal"&nbsp;
for his brawn, height, and in-game theatrics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.660
the six-foot-six-inch Lesley played&nbsp;
major league baseball from 1982 to 1985.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.640
Lesley moved to Japan in 1986, where he&nbsp;
played for two seasons before retiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.100
from baseball and becoming a Japanese television&nbsp;
personality. His larger-than-life stature and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.820
persona made him a fan favorite on the&nbsp;
Japanese game show "Takeshi's Castle."

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:16.620
In the 1990s, Lesley began appearing in&nbsp;
Hollywood films as well. He was best-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.940
for the roles that reflected aspects of his&nbsp;
personal life, like Niven in "Mr. Baseball"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:25.380
and John "Blackout" Gatling in "Little&nbsp;
Big League." Lesley began to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:31.140
declining health in the 2010s. After his kidneys&nbsp;
failed, he was placed on dialysis and moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:38.040
into a nursing home. On April 28, 2013, Brad&nbsp;
Lesley suffered a heart attack and died at 54.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:42.540
Alberta Watson spent her early adulthood&nbsp;
carving a career in Canadian television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:46.140
and movie productions, but soon&nbsp;
plunged into Hollywood work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.680
landed meaty parts in a variety&nbsp;
of films and television shows.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.942
"I don't tolerate any weaknesses.&nbsp;
That's not how I work."

00:10:53.942 --> 00:10:58.860
In the critically-acclaimed transgender musical&nbsp;
"Hedwig and the Angry Inch," Watson played the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:03.420
loving mother of Hansel "Hedwig" Schmidt.&nbsp;
Her dark turn as the incestuous mother in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.200
David O. Russell's directorial debut,&nbsp;
"Spanking the Monkey," stood as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.160
her favorite roles. She also appeared&nbsp;
in "The Sweet Hereafter" and "Hackers."

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:17.340
Watson's last film role was 2009's "Helen." She&nbsp;
ended her TV career in 2012 with them,.recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:22.380
role of Senator Madeline Pierce on "Nikita."&nbsp;
Watson was first diagnosed with lymphoma in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:28.973
1998, and died of complications from&nbsp;
cancer on March 21, 2015 at age 60.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.960
Throughout her elementary school years, Amanda&nbsp;
Peterson starred in local theater productions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:38.220
her hometown of Greeley, Colorado. It didn't&nbsp;
take long for Peterson to score an agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.500
and after guest appearances on hit '80s shows&nbsp;
like "Silver Spoons" and "Father Murphy," Peterson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:48.060
starred on the big screen in "Explorers,"&nbsp;
alongside River Phoenix and Ethan Hawke.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.640
It was Peterson's role as the popular cheerleader&nbsp;
Cindy Mancini in 1986's "Can't Buy Me Love"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:58.140
that endeared her to audiences. Although&nbsp;
she appeared destined for meteoric fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:02.753
Peterson's blazing Hollywood&nbsp;
career seemed to fizzle by 1994.

00:12:02.753 --> 00:12:04.150
"Work's not really my thing."

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.126
She retired from acting and&nbsp;
returned to life in Colorado.

00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:11.580
Peterson struggled to navigate through her&nbsp;
post-celebrity life. She turned to drugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:16.440
and soon developed a severe addiction. After&nbsp;
several arrests, Peterson attempted to regain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:20.880
her life. Friends told the Denver Post that in&nbsp;
her later days, she spoke of getting back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.520
acting — she dreamed of opening a studio for&nbsp;
young, aspiring actors. Amanda Peterson died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:33.201
of an accidental morphine overdose on July 3,&nbsp;
2015, three days short of her 44th birthday.

00:12:33.201 --> 00:12:37.680
Bridgette Andersen's name may not ring&nbsp;
a bell, but by the early '80s, she had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.760
appeared in a plethora of commercials.&nbsp;
After several TV guest appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.460
Andersen landed the starring role in 1982's&nbsp;
"Savannah Smiles," playing Savannah Driscoll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.800
a young runaway who befriends two convicts.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.640
Andersen received a Young Artist&nbsp;
Award nomination for her work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.540
appeared to be a star on the rise, but&nbsp;
acting roles dried up by the time she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:02.100
reached her teenage years. Her last film&nbsp;
appearance came in 1987's "Too Much."

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:08.820
Bridgette Andersen died on May 18, 1997,&nbsp;
of an opioid overdose at 21 years old.

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:13.980
In the early 1990s, Christopher Lee Pettiet's&nbsp;
classic good looks and natural talent helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:18.960
him land multiple television appearances on&nbsp;
shows like "Doogie Howser, M.D." and "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:23.340
The Next Generation." His early film roles&nbsp;
included "Point Break" and "Sandman."

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:27.600
But Pettiet is best known for his performance&nbsp;
as the middle brother, Zach Crandell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.000
in the 1989 cult classic "Don't Tell Mom the&nbsp;
Babysitter's Dead." The black comedy initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:37.560
tanked at the box office and was panned by&nbsp;
critics, but eventually gained a large cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:42.180
following. Pettiet earned teen heartthrob&nbsp;
status and steady work due to his role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:47.640
including parts in the ABC series "The Young&nbsp;
Riders" and the 1996 indie film "Boys."

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:54.405
On April 12, 2000, Pettiet died from an&nbsp;
accidental drug overdose. He was 24 years old.

00:13:54.604 --> 00:13:59.580
Ashleigh Aston Moore is best known for&nbsp;
her role as Chrissy in 1995's "Now and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:03.960
Then," playing the young counterpart to Rita&nbsp;
Wilson's adult character in flashback scenes.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.505
"Chrissy. Let go of your inhibitions&nbsp;
before you dry up like a prune."

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:11.419
"What's that supposed to mean?"

00:14:11.419 --> 00:14:15.600
"Now and Then" stood out as a female-driven&nbsp;
film full of rich character development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.500
painting a realistic portrait of the&nbsp;
tween years and becoming a favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.780
shared movie experience for many young girls.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:25.980
Like so many child actors, Moore began&nbsp;
appearing in commercials when she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:30.240
barely out of diapers. But her acting career&nbsp;
began to fade after her turn in "Now and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:35.400
Then." She made her last screen appearance in&nbsp;
1997, in an episode of "Touched by An Angel."

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:39.600
Moore opted out of the Hollywood life,&nbsp;
and moved to Vancouver, British Columbia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:44.520
Canada. According to friends, she'd developed&nbsp;
an addiction to heroin while still in her teens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:47.820
which continued through her&nbsp;
adulthood. At the age of 26,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:53.400
Ashleigh Aston Moore died of an accidental&nbsp;
heroin overdose on December 10, 2007.

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:56.820
Growing up in the projects of the&nbsp;
East Village in New York City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:00.420
Harold Hunter developed a passion&nbsp;
for skateboarding. He spent most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.980
of his time skating in city parks, where he&nbsp;
quickly gained attention from skate apparel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:10.380
companies. Hunter was featured in Thrasher&nbsp;
magazine in 1989 and sponsored by Zoo York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:14.340
He snagged modeling jobs for prominent&nbsp;
fashion companies like Tommy Hilfiger.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:19.380
Hunter symbolized the New York City skate kids'&nbsp;
culture, and remained the reigning "king" of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:24.360
city's skate parks well into adulthood. After&nbsp;
starring as Harold in "Kids," Hunter appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:28.620
in an episode of "New York Undercover"&nbsp;
and held minor roles in independent films.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:34.620
On February 17, 2006, Harold Hunter&nbsp;
died at the age of 31 of myocardial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.800
infarction — a heart attack induced by cocaine.

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:43.140
Best known for his roles as the woefully-named&nbsp;
"Half Sack" on the FX drama "Sons of Anarchy"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:46.320
and "Chili" on Fox's "The O.C.,"&nbsp;
Johnny Lewis left the Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:52.380
community reeling in shock on September 26,&nbsp;
2012. He killed 81-year-old Cathy Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:56.760
a longtime friend to artists and the&nbsp;
landlady of the Writer's Villa. Lewis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.760 --> 00:16:01.740
who had previously rented a suite from Davis&nbsp;
in 2009, had just moved back into the home.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:05.340
After killing Davis, Lewis fell to&nbsp;
his death from the villa's roof.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:08.760
In the months leading up to his&nbsp;
death, Lewis had exhibited strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:12.480
violent behavior and had spent time&nbsp;
in Twin Towers Correctional Facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:17.520
for committing several acts of unprovoked&nbsp;
violence. He experienced constant legal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:22.560
mental health issues between 2009&nbsp;
and his death at age 28 in 2012.

00:16:22.966 --> 00:16:27.120
Justin Pierce dropped out of high school&nbsp;
and became part of the skateboarding scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:32.040
in New York City in the early '90s. Trouble&nbsp;
with drugs and criminal activity peppered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:36.840
Pierce's teen years, but a role in the&nbsp;
1995 film "Kids" would provide an escape.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:41.220
Photographer-turned-director Larry Clark&nbsp;
discovered the 19-year-old skateboarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:45.360
in Washington Square Park with his friends.&nbsp;
The fledgling director offered Pierce his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:50.328
first starring film role as obnoxious Casper,&nbsp;
the best friend of "Kids" protagonist Telly.

00:16:50.328 --> 00:16:54.813
"They way I see it? My outlook on the situation?"
"Mr. Wizard."

00:16:54.813 --> 00:16:56.943
"It's like getting fame,&nbsp;
you know what I'm saying?"

00:16:56.943 --> 00:17:01.140
The&nbsp;&nbsp; controversial film resonated with&nbsp;
teenage audiences and critics alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:05.160
and Pierce won an Independent Spirit&nbsp;
Award for his role. He continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:09.000
connect with viewers through his authentic&nbsp;
portrayal of troubled teens, going on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:13.920
star in "A Brother's Kiss," "Next Friday,"&nbsp;
and other independent films. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:17.880
Pierce's life issues followed him&nbsp;
to Hollywood. While in Las Vegas to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:23.820
promote "Next Friday," the then 25-year-old&nbsp;
actor took his own life on July 10, 2000.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:28.800
Best known for playing Geoffrey Barone on&nbsp;
"Everybody Loves Raymond," Sawyer Sweeten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.540
was cast with his twin brother, Sullivan, at&nbsp;
just 23 months old. Though the twins were cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:39.180
together on the Ray Romano vehicle, Sawyer went on&nbsp;
to land a film role without his brother. In 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:44.520
the actor played a young Frank McKlusky in&nbsp;
"Frank McKlusky, C.I." The film didn't fare well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:49.260
at the box office and was panned by critics, but&nbsp;
Sawyer proved he could act on the silver screen.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:54.540
Sawyer grew up in the limelight, and by all&nbsp;
accounts was a funny and kind kid. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.540 --> 00:18:01.846
things came to an abrupt end when he took his&nbsp;
own life on April 23, 2015, at just 19 years old.

